# 10. DEMON AI — PURSUIT SYSTEM

The demon must have a reliable and convincing pursuit system.

Use a finite state machine.

Possible states:

1. DORMANT
2. OBSERVING
3. TURNING
4. APPROACHING
5. CHASING
6. NEAR_PLAYER
7. ATTACKING
8. BLOCKED_BY_RIVER
9. ENDING_STARE

## 10.1 Initial state

The demon begins in OBSERVING.

It is visible at a distance.

It does not move initially.

After the designed delay, it turns toward the player.

Then it transitions into CHASING.

## 10.2 Chase behavior

The demon must:

- Continuously pursue the player.
- Maintain a believable direction.
- Avoid sudden teleportation.
- Avoid passing through major terrain obstacles.
- Stay grounded in the world coordinate system.
- Maintain correct scale.
- Use smooth movement.
- Adjust movement speed based on distance and gameplay pacing.

The AI must never use unrealistic instant position changes.

## 10.3 Chase balance

The demon should be slightly faster than a normal walking player.

The sprinting player should be able to make meaningful progress toward the river.

The player should feel threatened throughout the chase.

The demon should be able to catch the player if the player makes poor movement decisions or fails to maintain progress.

Do not make survival mathematically impossible.

Test the complete route.

## 10.4 Catch detection

Create a reliable collision or distance-based catch system.

When the demon reaches the player:

- Trigger a brief attack animation.
- Use a short camera reaction.
- Play a disturbing audio cue.
- Fade to black.
- Display a death screen.

Do not use an excessively long or graphically explicit death animation.

## 10.5 River boundary

This is a mandatory gameplay rule.

When the demon reaches the near riverbank, it must stop.

It must never cross the river.

It must never swim after the player.

It must never teleport to the opposite bank.

It remains on the near side of the river and stares at the player.

The demon's state becomes BLOCKED_BY_RIVER.

After the player reaches the opposite bank, transition to ENDING_STARE.

This behavior must be deterministic and reliable.

